WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
REMEMBER DISCOVERY?:
Do you remember the discovery channel? For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:09.600
many of us, the discovery channel was a classic&nbsp;
portion of our childhood. Basically, if you had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.600
grown out of the Nickelodeon and Disney phase, you&nbsp;
were probably watching the Discovery Channel. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:22.320
had everything from How It’s Made, Gold Rush, Cash&nbsp;
Cab, and Deadliest Catch to Mythbusters, Dirty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.500
Jobs, Planet Earth, and Naked and Afraid. They&nbsp;
also had random one off specials like Nik Wallenda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:35.400
crossing the Grand Canyon. Also, who could forget&nbsp;
shark week. Every year, shark week almost felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.350
like a worldwide event like the World Cup or&nbsp;
the Olympics. Whether you went to get a hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:47.040
cut or went to Home Depot, you’d probably overhear&nbsp;
people talking about shark week. But, since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.920
much has changed for the Discovery Channel.&nbsp;
For one, many people don’t even have regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.800
cable TV or satellite TV. Instead, they simply&nbsp;
have a hand full of subscriptions like Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.920
and Disney+ along with maybe YouTube Premium. The&nbsp;
Discovery Channel did try to ride this new trend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.920
by launching their own streaming service called&nbsp;
Discovery+ in late 2020. But, this hasn’t worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:17.280
out all that well. Sure, they were able to get to&nbsp;
about 22 million subscribers, but this is nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:23.940
in comparison to their status a decade ago. In&nbsp;
2010, for example, MythBusters alone pulled in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:30.480
85.8 million views within just the first 9 months&nbsp;
of the year. The Discovery Channel was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:38.220
so big that they were their own public company&nbsp;
worth as much as $20 billion. But, more recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:44.280
the Discovery Channel agreed to complete a merger&nbsp;
with WarnerMedia, and this is how their new stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:50.820
has performed. Their popularity graph isn’t much&nbsp;
better either, in fact, it’s actually worse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:58.740
Since the 2000s, search volume for the Discovery&nbsp;
Channel has literally dropped by 90 to 95%. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:04.140
here’s the rise and fall of one of the most&nbsp;
classic media businesses of our entier generation.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.160
DISCOVERING THE ORIGIN:&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.620
Taking a look back, the story of the discovery&nbsp;
channel dates all the way back to 1982 to a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.900
named John Hendricks. John had a pretty average&nbsp;
background. His father owned a local contractor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:24.660
business while his mother worked as a clerk at&nbsp;
the city government. So, like most people, John’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:30.120
obvious career choice was to go to college and get&nbsp;
a job. John ended up attending the University of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:35.340
Alabama where he studied history. This was also&nbsp;
when John started experimenting with his creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.920
side. You see, while John attended college, he&nbsp;
had a part time job working in the audio visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:46.200
department, and this is where he really fell&nbsp;
in love with producing documentaries. John felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.400
that quality documentaries were something&nbsp;
that the American public severely lacked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.800
but he also wasn’t crazy enough to dive straight&nbsp;
into the media business. Instead, he got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.720
traditional job at his university after graduation&nbsp;
where he led community and government relations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.420
He would eventually transition to the University&nbsp;
of Maryland where he did the same job, but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:11.820
time, he would also work on an entrepreneurial&nbsp;
project. He cofounded a fundraising consulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:17.520
company that focused on publishing academic&nbsp;
newsletters. The company never rose to insane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:23.400
heights, but it looks like it gave John enough&nbsp;
confidence to take a gamble with media. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.740
in 1982, John would found Cable Educational&nbsp;
Network, and as you would guess, the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:34.380
focused on providing documentary programming to&nbsp;
cable broadcasters. The big jump forward didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:39.900
come till 1985 though when bigger players&nbsp;
also become interested in the space. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:46.260
BBC was likely the most well known of these,&nbsp;
but they were by no means the only one. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:51.780
players became investors in a new project led&nbsp;
by John called the Discovery Channel. Initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:58.080
this was just supposed to be a subsidiary of Cable&nbsp;
Educational Network, but as funding grew larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.180
and larger, John would shift his entire focus&nbsp;
to the Discovery Channel. Soon enough, he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:07.920
actually change the name of the parent company&nbsp;
to Discovery Communications Inc, and that marked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.320
the beginning of network that we’re all familiar&nbsp;
with today. At first, the company only broadcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:21.780
12 hours a day between 3 pm and 3 am to a modest&nbsp;
156,000 households. But, everything they aired as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:28.080
a banger. 75% of their content had never been seen&nbsp;
by an American audience and much of it covered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:33.480
fascinating educational content. This included&nbsp;
cultural documentaries, wildlife documentaries and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:39.900
science and history specials as well. They even&nbsp;
had some Soviet programming. Very early on, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:45.480
also experimented with Shark Week which quickly&nbsp;
turned into an annual event. Given that Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:51.180
couldn’t get this content anywhere else, the&nbsp;
Discovery channel exploded in popularity. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:57.780
within 5 years of launch, their reach would grow&nbsp;
from 156,000 households to 50 million households.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:04.380
John had clearly hit a goldmine and all that was&nbsp;
left was to double down and build on this success.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:08.580
THE DISCOVERY EMPIRE:
At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:14.760
first, the next steps were obvious and clear.&nbsp;
Just keep doing exactly what you were doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:20.700
but at a larger scale. This meant expanding to an&nbsp;
international audience and launching and acquiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:26.940
new networks, and this is exactly what Discovery&nbsp;
did. They expanded to the UK in 1989 and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:31.920
followed this up with an expansion to Asia and&nbsp;
Latin America. Something you may not know about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:37.380
Discovery is that they actually owned a bunch of&nbsp;
our other favorite channels as well. This started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:43.020
off with an acquisition of TLC in 1991 and quickly&nbsp;
grew to encompass Animal Planet, Planet Green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:48.960
the Military Chanel, and FitTV. Discovery also&nbsp;
ran a number of popular websites as well including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.240
Howstuffworks.com and treehugger.com. While they&nbsp;
had their hands in so many different genres and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:06:00.318
mediums, the core mission of the company always&nbsp;
rang true which was to be “The number-one non&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.318 --> 00:06:06.600
fiction media company.” All of this climaxed in&nbsp;
2001 when the Discovery Channel became the world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:12.120
most widely distributed television brand. They&nbsp;
were even bigger than Netflix is today reaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:18.000
300 million households in over 180 countries. At&nbsp;
one point, it was estimated that their cumulative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:24.000
viewers had even crossed 1 billion people per&nbsp;
year. They had made it to the absolute top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:29.160
but this made the following question very&nbsp;
difficult to answer: where do they go from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:34.860
here? Well, the correct answer would have been to&nbsp;
just keep doing what they were doing and be happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:40.440
being a mature media network. But, investors&nbsp;
nor executives were exactly pleased with just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:46.800
that. They wanted more growth, more viewers, and&nbsp;
of course more money. And given that Discovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.940
had peaked out in the non fiction market,&nbsp;
there was only one way to continue growing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:57.240
aim for a broader audience. This meant adding in&nbsp;
more elements of reality TV and focusing more on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:03.360
the storyline than the facts. In the beginning,&nbsp;
this actually worked out great, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:08.880
you could even argue that this is exactly what&nbsp;
Discovery was previously missing. While purely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.260
nonfictional content was great, this&nbsp;
extra element made the content phenomenal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:18.780
ad this gave rise to the golden age of&nbsp;
Discovery. Show like Mythbusters, Dirty Jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:24.720
and Deadliest Catch had this perfect mix of&nbsp;
nonfiction and fiction. Mythbusters, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.660
centered on the scientific process, physics, and&nbsp;
engineering, but it wasn’t limited to just that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:37.080
It also had personalities, drama, emotions, and&nbsp;
most importantly, people that you could relate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:42.480
with. Mike Rowe from Dirty Jobs was likely one of&nbsp;
the most down to earth people to have ever been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:43.920
on TV, though he has started to take some heat&nbsp;
for his anti union stances more recently. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:49.680
golden period didn’t last forever though. In&nbsp;
late 2007, Discovery announced that they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.920
gonna go public in 2008. This was terrible timing&nbsp;
given that it was during the heart of the great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:02.220
recession, and it wasn’t that surprising&nbsp;
that the IPO didn’t go all that well. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:07.740
this lackluster IPO was just the beginning&nbsp;
of Discovery’s long and painful descent.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.880
DISCOVERING NEW LOWS:&nbsp;

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:17.400
Now that Discovery was public, pressure from&nbsp;
investors was larger than ever, and they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:22.980
legally obligated to act in the best interest&nbsp;
of investors. Discovery was already headed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:28.260
the direction of adding in too much reality&nbsp;
and not enough science, but the IPO simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:33.960
solidified this trend. The show that most people&nbsp;
point to as the epitome of Discovery's decline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:39.360
is American Chopper. The show focused on a group&nbsp;
of people who set out to create the world’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.340
unique motorcycles. While this was indeed an&nbsp;
interesting premise, it had virtually nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:51.000
to do with science, education, or learning.&nbsp;
It was a reality show, a successful one, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:57.060
but was it something that aligned with Discovery’s&nbsp;
mission? Not at all. While the show garnered a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:02.520
bunch of viewers, it was completely different&nbsp;
demographic. Fortunately, classic Discovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:09.060
viewers had classic shows to turn back to right?&nbsp;
Well, yes and no. The classic shows were indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:15.060
still being produced and aired, but these too&nbsp;
were missing something. Shark Week, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:19.680
starting receiving a bunch of criticism that it&nbsp;
was filled with a bunch of misinformation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:25.320
blatantly false science. Similarly, Mythbusters&nbsp;
was drifting away from science and experiments to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:32.340
action and explosions. We saw fewer clips of Jamie&nbsp;
or Grant explaining Newton’s laws and more clips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:38.220
of the crew blowing up random crap just for fun.&nbsp;
Now, don’t get me wrong here, watching them blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:44.340
up random crap was quite entertaining in itself,&nbsp;
but you still couldn’t deny that the show had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:50.040
somehow lost what made it so great. Soon enough,&nbsp;
these shows were not only losing their souls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:54.840
but they were getting cancelled. This didn’t&nbsp;
necessarily mean that the shows were doing bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:59.340
per se, but they had simply reached their&nbsp;
natural ends. Dirty Jobs, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:05.640
wrapped up in 2012 while Mythbusters wrapped up in&nbsp;
2016. Usually, there would’ve been plenty of shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:11.100
that these viewers could have switched too, but&nbsp;
this time, all that was left was the reality crap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:17.820
And overtime, the core discovery viewer simply&nbsp;
tuned out. This downtrend was simply accelerated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:23.700
by macro conditions within the media industry.&nbsp;
The trend wasn’t to get cable or satellite TV.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:32.040
The trend was to ditch cable and satellite TV.&nbsp;
In 2015, 76% of Americans had traditional TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:40.140
but by 2021, only 56% of Americans had traditional&nbsp;
TV. Meanwhile, platforms like Netflix and Hulu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:44.880
were skyrocketing in popularity. Netflix, for&nbsp;
example, has grown from 50 million subscribers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:51.480
to over 200 million subscribers in the last 7 to&nbsp;
8 years. And the worst part about all of this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:55.680
that the majority of the people leaving cable&nbsp;
for Netflix was the more generic audience who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:00.540
was interested in the reality shows. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
the people that were left behind were the classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:06.420
Discovery viewers. Yet, Discovery was spending all&nbsp;
their efforts chasing after the rapidly shrinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:11.880
reality audience. And at this point, there&nbsp;
was only one way that this story could end.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:16.080
DISCOVERING THE FUTURE:&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:21.540
Looking forward, while Discovery is by no means&nbsp;
as large as they once used to be, they’re still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:27.780
a sizeable media organization. I mean 22 million&nbsp;
subscribers is nothing to scoff at. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:32.760
the merger with Warner Media has boosted their&nbsp;
total subscribers all the way to over 90 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:38.460
With this new catalyst, it seems like it’s just a&nbsp;
matter of time until the new Discovery is able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:43.980
rival Netflix and Disney with their 200 million&nbsp;
plus subscriber counts. But, while Discovery has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:49.500
a solid chance to become a media giant once again,&nbsp;
the Discovery that we all knew and loved is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:55.200
very much dead. This second wave of growth will&nbsp;
almost completely be fueled by reality shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:02.160
like Trixie Motel, 90 day fiance, Alone, and Ghost&nbsp;
Adventrues. And given the merger with WarnerMedia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:07.380
it’s unlikely that Discovery will ever return to&nbsp;
what we remember it as. They’ve simply shifted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:12.960
serving the broader entertainment market, and&nbsp;
that’s what happened to Discovery. Do you guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:19.140
prefer the old Discovery or the new Discovery?&nbsp;
Comment that down below. Also, drop a like if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:23.850
miss the old Discovery. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:27.540
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

